# stim-concat

**Participant-specific concatenated stimulus videos, with frame-exact timelines, for behavioural experiments.**

`stim-concat` turns a folder of stimulus files into one ready-to-play video per
participant, together with an annotated timeline of every event in that video.
Because the whole session is a single video file, an experiment can be run with
*any* recording setup — eye-tracker, joystick or gamepad rating system,
physiological amplifier, or a custom application — without needing stimulus
presentation software to drive it. The timeline is what you use afterwards to
align your recordings to the stimuli.

```
Stimulus folder
      ↓
Assignment generator   ──►  participant_assignments.csv  (+ metadata sidecar)
      ↓
Stimulus builder
      ↓
Participant001.mp4  +  Participant001_timeline.csv / .xlsx  +  Participant001_settings.json
```

---

## Why the timeline can be trusted

The point of this tool is synchronisation, so the timeline is not an estimate.
Three implementation choices make it exact:

1. **Durations are snapped to whole frames** before anything is rendered, so a
   15.372 s clip at 30 fps becomes exactly 461 frames.
2. **Every segment is pinned with `-frames:v N`.** An earlier version used `-t`
   alone and accumulated four frames of drift (~133 ms) across a 44-second
   build, because each segment rounded independently.
3. **Intermediate segments carry uncompressed PCM audio** and are joined with a
   stream copy. Lossy codecs pad to their own frame size (~21 ms for AAC), which
   would desynchronise audio a little more with every segment; audio is encoded
   once, at the join.

The test suite asserts this: `_video_frames(output) == round(timeline.duration * fps)`.

## Install

```bash
pip install stim-concat
```

Nothing else is required. FFmpeg is supplied by the `imageio-ffmpeg` wheel; a
system FFmpeg is used instead when one is on `PATH`. Check an installation with:

```bash
stim-concat doctor
```

On Debian/Ubuntu the graphical wizard also needs Tk: `sudo apt install python3-tk`.
The command line interface works without it.

## Use

### The wizard

```bash
stim-concat gui        # or just: stim-concat
```

Five pages: choose the stimulus folder → generate assignments → configure
fixation, instructions, presentation and video → preview timelines and build →
review the summary. Instruction and fixation screens are previewed live, exactly
as they will be rendered.

### The command line

```bash
stim-concat scan stimuli/
stim-concat algorithms -v
stim-concat assign stimuli/ -n 24 -k 8 --algorithm balanced_random --seed 42
stim-concat preview participant_assignments.csv stimuli/ --participant P001
stim-concat build participant_assignments.csv stimuli/ output/
```

Settings are a plain JSON file, so a build is fully scriptable:

```bash
stim-concat config -o settings.json      # write the defaults, then edit
stim-concat build assignments.csv stimuli/ output/ --config settings.json
```

### As a library

```python
from stim_concat import scan_folder, run_algorithm, BuildConfig, build_all

stimuli = scan_folder("stimuli/", id_pattern=r"(?P<id>\d+)")
assignment = run_algorithm("bibd", stimuli.ids, 21, 5, seed=42)
assignment.to_csv("participant_assignments.csv")

config = BuildConfig()
config.video.width, config.video.height = 1920, 1080
config.timeline.trial_sequence = ["fixation", "instruction", "stimulus"]

report = build_all(assignment, stimuli, config, "output/")
print(report.describe())
```

## Stage 1 — assignment

Supported inputs are video (`.mp4 .mov .avi .mkv .m4v .webm .mpg .wmv`), images
(`.jpg .png .bmp .tif .webp .gif`), audio (`.mp3 .wav .m4a .aac .flac .ogg`) and
text (`.txt .md`). Adding a format is one line:

```python
from stim_concat.core.scanner import register_format
register_format(".mxf", "video")
```

Stimulus IDs come from the filename via a regular expression, so
`clip_070_sad.mp4` can yield `clip_070_sad`, `070` or `sad` depending on the
pattern you choose.

### Built-in algorithms

| Key | What it guarantees |
| --- | --- |
| `simple_random` | Independent draws; repeats possible |
| `random_without_replacement` | Distinct stimuli within a participant |
| `balanced_random` | Every stimulus used ⌊N/v⌋ or ⌈N/v⌉ times overall |
| `block_randomisation` | Equal numbers from each condition; block order randomised |
| `latin_square` | Each stimulus once per serial position; Williams option balances carry-over |
| `bibd` | Equal replication *and* equal pair co-occurrence |
| `constrained_pseudorandom` | Balanced, plus limits on category runs and position repeats |

`bibd` reports honestly. An exact design exists only when `r = bk/v` and
`λ = r(k−1)/(v−1)` are whole numbers; the search recovers known designs (the
Fano plane, the projective plane of order 3) and otherwise says so and returns
the most balanced near-design, with replication still exactly balanced.

### The algorithm *is* the script

Each algorithm is a standalone Python file, shown in an editor inside the
application. What you read is what runs. Edit it, press Generate, and the edited
source is stored in the sheet's metadata sidecar so the result stays
reproducible. Any file defining

```python
def assign(stimuli, n_participants, n_per_participant, rng, params):
    return [[...], ...]     # one list of stimulus IDs per participant
```

can be dropped into `~/.stim-concat/algorithms/` and appears in the list. Always
draw from the supplied `rng`, never the global `random`, or seeds will not
reproduce.

> Running an algorithm executes Python, exactly as running an analysis script
> does. Only run scripts you trust.

## Stage 2 — building

Per trial you choose the order of `fixation`, `instruction`, `stimulus` and
`blank`; opening and closing screens wrap the session. Configurable: fixation
duration, size, thickness, colour, background and position; instruction text,
duration, font, size, colour, alignment and per-stimulus overrides; stimulus
position (nine anchors plus pixel nudges), scaling (`contain`/`cover`/`stretch`/
`none`), background colour, still-image duration; resolution, frame rate, codec,
CRF or bitrate, preset, and audio codec/bitrate/sample rate/channels. Source
audio is preserved by default.

Instruction and fixation screens are drawn with Pillow rather than FFmpeg's
`drawtext`, because many FFmpeg builds — including the static binaries inside
`imageio-ffmpeg` — are compiled without libfreetype and have no `drawtext`
filter at all. Drawing in Python also means text is wrapped by measured pixel
width and shrunk if needed, so it never clips at unusual resolutions.

### Outputs

| File | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `P001.mp4` | The concatenated session |
| `P001_timeline.csv` | Every event: index, start, end, duration, type, trial, stimulus ID, description, source file |
| `P001_timeline.xlsx` | The same, colour-coded by event type |
| `P001_settings.json` | Full configuration, stimulus list, FFmpeg version, timestamp |
| `build_summary.csv` / `.md` | Per-participant status across the whole build |

```csv
event_index,start_s,end_s,duration_s,event_type,trial,stimulus_id,description,source_file
0,0.0,8.0,8.0,instruction,,,Opening instructions,
1,8.0,9.0,1.0,fixation,1,,Fixation cross,
2,9.0,12.0,3.0,instruction,1,070,Default instruction,
3,12.0,27.372,15.372,stimulus,1,070,Video,clip_070.mp4
```

For a worked example of cutting a continuous recording into per-stimulus
epochs, see [`docs/timeline-alignment.md`](docs/timeline-alignment.md).

## Reproducibility

A study can be reconstructed from two files. The assignment metadata sidecar
holds the algorithm, seed, parameters, the exact script source and a fingerprint
of the stimulus pool; `*_settings.json` holds every rendering parameter plus the
FFmpeg version. Re-running with the same seed reproduces the sheet exactly.

## Development

```bash
git clone https://github.com/stim-concat/stim-concat
cd stim-concat
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest                    # 99 tests; rendering tests skip if FFmpeg is absent
ruff check src tests
```

Layout: `core/` (scanner, config, screens, timeline, renderer, exporters,
pipeline), `assignment/` (data model, registry, algorithm scripts), `gui/`
(wizard shell, one module per page), `cli.py`. Assignment algorithms, timeline
elements and exporters extend independently of one another.

To build a standalone application: `pyinstaller packaging/stim-concat.spec`.

